3. Data Model

The solution introduces one new custom object to store the summaries and one new custom field on the existing Case object to track the last transfer time.

3.1 Custom Object: Case_Transfer_Summary__c

Stores the generated summaries related to a Case.

| Field Name (Label)           | API Name             | Data Type          | Details                                                                                                  |
| ---------------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Case Transfer Summary Name   | Name               | Auto Number        | Standard name field, configured as an auto-number CTS-{000000}.                                        |
| Case                         | Case__c            | Master-Detail(Case) | Master-detail relationship to the Case this summary belongs to.                                          |
| Summary                      | Summary__c         | Long Text Area (32768) | Stores the rich text summary generated by the LLM.                                                       |
| Generated At                 | Generated_At__c    | DateTime           | Timestamp of when the summary was successfully generated and saved.                                      |
| Generated By                 | Generated_By__c    | Lookup(User)       | The user who initiated the case owner change that triggered this summary generation.                     |
| LLM Request Id               | LLM_Request_Id__c  | Text (255), Unique | An optional field to store a unique identifier from the LLM API call for traceability and debugging.     |

3.2 Custom Field: Case.Last_Owner_Change_Timestamp__c

A field on the Case object to track when the owner was last changed. This can be used for reporting or to prevent re-summarization within a short time window if needed in future enhancements.

| Field Name (Label)             | API Name                        | Data Type | Details                                                                                   |
| ------------------------------ | ------------------------------- | --------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Last Owner Change Timestamp    | Last_Owner_Change_Timestamp__c| DateTime  | Stores the timestamp of the last owner change. Updated by the CaseTriggerHandler.       |

4. Apex Trigger and Handler Logic

The entry point for the automation is a trigger on the Case object. A trigger handler pattern is used to keep the trigger itself logic-less and to facilitate testing and maintenance.
  - Trigger: CaseTrigger runs on after update.
  - Handler: CaseTriggerHandler contains the business logic.

Logic Flow:

1.  The CaseTrigger fires after a Case record is updated.
2.  It delegates processing to the CaseTriggerHandler.handleAfterUpdate() method, passing Trigger.new and Trigger.oldMap.
3.  The handleAfterUpdate method iterates through the updated cases.
4.  For each case, it checks for the following entry criteria:
  - OwnerId has changed.
  - The new OwnerId is a User ID (starts with '005'), not a Queue ID (starts with '00G'). This ensures summaries are only generated for agent-to-agent transfers.
5.  If the criteria are met, the Case ID is added to a list. The Last_Owner_Change_Timestamp__c field is also updated to the current time.
6.  After checking all cases, if the list of eligible Case IDs is not empty, the handler enqueues a single SummarizationQueueable job, passing the list of Case IDs to its constructor.
7.  The handler also performs a DML update on the Cases to save the Last_Owner_Change_Timestamp__c. This is done separately from the trigger's original transaction context to ensure the timestamp is set correctly.

This design is fully bulkified, processing up to 200 cases in a single transaction and launching only one Queueable job per transaction.

5. Asynchronous Processing (Queueable)

The SummarizationQueueable class handles the core logic asynchronously. This is critical for two reasons:
1.  Preventing Mixed DML/Callout Errors: A callout to an external service cannot be made after a DML operation in the same transaction. The trigger context involves a DML (the Case update). By moving the callout to a separate, asynchronous transaction, we avoid this limitation.
2.  Governor Limits & User Experience: The callout to the LLM may take several seconds. Performing this synchronously would lock the user's screen and could easily exceed the CPU time limit. The queueable approach ensures the user's action completes instantly, while the summarization happens in the background.

Logic Flow:
1.  The execute method of the queueable receives the list of Case IDs from the constructor.
2.  It instantiates the CaseSummarizationService.
3.  It calls the CaseSummarizationService.generateAndSaveSummaries(caseIds) method, delegating the main business logic to the service layer.
4.  A try-catch block wraps the call to the service to handle any potential exceptions during the process, ensuring the job doesn't fail silently. Errors are logged for administrative review.

6. Case Summarization Service Logic

The CaseSummarizationService class is the heart of the business logic. It is responsible for orchestrating the entire summarization process for a given set of Case IDs. This separation of concerns makes the code more modular and testable.

Logic Flow:
1.  Data Gathering: The service receives a list of Case IDs. It performs a single, bulk SOQL query to retrieve all necessary data for these cases. This includes:
  - Case fields: Id, Subject, Description.
  - Related records: The 5 most recent CaseComment records and the 3 most recent EmailMessage records (text body only). The query is structured to efficiently pull this related data in a sub-query.
2.  Prompt Construction: For each Case, the service assembles a detailed prompt. This prompt is carefully crafted to provide the LLM with sufficient context to generate a high-quality summary. It includes the case subject, description, and the text from the recent comments and emails.
3.  LLM Callout: It iterates through the prepared prompts. For each one, it calls the LlmApiCalloutService.getSummary(prompt) method.
4.  Response Handling: It receives the summary text back from the callout service.
5.  Record Creation: If a valid summary is returned, it constructs a new Case_Transfer_Summary__c record, populating all fields: Case__c, Summary__c, Generated_At__c, Generated_By__c (the user who initiated the job), and the LLM_Request_Id__c if available.
6.  Bulk DML: All the newly created Case_Transfer_Summary__c records are added to a list and inserted in a single DML operation at the end of the process to ensure bulkification.

7. API Callout Service Logic

The LlmApiCalloutService is a reusable, generic service responsible solely for the technical details of making the HTTP callout to the external LLM API. It abstracts away the complexities of HTTP requests and responses.

Logic Flow:
1.  Named Credential: The service uses a Named Credential (LLM_API) to manage the API endpoint URL and authentication. This is a security best practice, as it avoids hardcoding URLs and credentials in the code.
2.  Request Construction: It creates an HttpRequest object.
  - Sets the endpoint using the Named Credential (callout:LLM_API/summarize).
  - Sets the method to POST.
  - Sets headers, such as Content-Type: application/json.
  - Serializes the request body, which contains the prompt, into a JSON string.
3.  Send Request: It sends the request using Http.send(req).
4.  Response Handling:
  - It checks the HttpResponse.getStatusCode(). If it's not 200 (OK), it logs an error and returns null.
  - If the status is 200, it deserializes the JSON response body into a wrapper class (LlmApiResponse).
  - It extracts the summary text from the wrapper class and returns it.
5.  Error Handling: A try-catch block handles any CalloutException, logging the error and ensuring the service fails gracefully by returning null.

8. LWC Display Component

The caseTransferSummaryDisplay LWC provides the user interface for viewing the generated summary on the Case record page.
  - HTML (caseTransferSummaryDisplay.html):
  - Uses a lightning-card to provide a standard Salesforce look and feel with a title "Case Transfer Summary".
  - A template if:true={summary} directive ensures the component only renders if a summary has been loaded.
  - Displays the summary content using lightning-formatted-rich-text, which correctly renders any formatting from the summary.
  - Displays the generation timestamp and the user who triggered it for context.
  - A template if:true={error} handles and displays any errors encountered while fetching the data.
  - JavaScript (caseTransferSummaryDisplay.js):
  - Uses the @api recordId decorator to automatically receive the ID of the Case record it's placed on.
  - Uses the @wire decorator to call the getLatestSummary method in the CaseTransferSummaryController Apex class, passing the recordId.
  - The wiredSummary function handles the response from the wire service. If data is returned, it's assigned to the summary property. If an error occurs, it's assigned to the error property. This is a reactive process; the component will re-render automatically when the data arrives.
  - Apex Controller (CaseTransferSummaryController.cls):
  - Contains a single @AuraEnabled(cacheable=true) method, getLatestSummary.
  - The cacheable=true annotation is a performance best practice, allowing the Lightning Data Service to cache the results.
  - The method accepts a caseId as a parameter.
  - It performs a SOQL query to find the most recent Case_Transfer_Summary__c record for the given caseId (ORDER BY CreatedDate DESC LIMIT 1).
  - It returns the found record (or null if none exists) to the LWC.
  - The class is defined with sharing to enforce the running user's sharing settings. They can only see summaries for cases they have access to.

9. Security and Permissions

Security is managed through a dedicated Permission Set and by following Apex security best practices.
  - Permission Set (CaseTransferSummaryAccess):
  - Object Permissions: Grants Read access to the Case_Transfer_Summary__c object.
  - Field Permissions: Grants Read access to all fields on the Case_Transfer_Summary__c object.
  - Apex Class Access: Grants execute access to the CaseTransferSummaryController so users with this permission set can see the LWC.
  - This permission set should be assigned to all users who need to view the case summaries (e.g., support agents, managers).
  - Apex Security (with sharing / without sharing):
  - CaseTransferSummaryController: Declared with sharing to ensure that users can only view summaries for Case records they have access to, respecting the organization's sharing model.
  - CaseTriggerHandler, SummarizationQueueable, CaseSummarizationService: Declared without sharing. This is a deliberate design choice. The summary generation is a system-level process that should execute successfully regardless of the specific user's permissions on the Case_Transfer_Summary__c object. The user who changes the owner might not have Create permission on the summary object, but the system needs to create the record on their behalf. Security is enforced at the point of display (the LWC controller).

10. Test Plan

Each component of the solution has a dedicated test class to ensure correctness, security, and bulkification. The overall code coverage will exceed the required 75%.
  - CaseTriggerHandlerTest.cls:
  - testOwnerChangeToUser_Single: Verifies the queueable is enqueued for a single record update where the owner changes to a user.
  - testOwnerChangeToUser_Bulk: Verifies a single queueable is enqueued for a bulk update of 200 cases.
  - testOwnerChangeToQueue: Verifies the queueable is NOT enqueued when the owner changes to a queue.
  - testNoOwnerChange: Verifies the queueable is NOT enqueued when other fields on the case are updated.
  - SummarizationQueueableTest.cls:
  - Uses Test.startTest() and Test.stopTest() to execute the queueable job synchronously within the test context.
  - Verifies that the CaseSummarizationService.generateAndSaveSummaries method is called with the correct Case IDs.